## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph traversal algorithms, BFS implementation, and pathfinding skills - critical for autonomous vehicle navigation systems and route planning applications.

## Expected Answer
The candidate should implement BFS using a queue, maintain a visited set to avoid cycles, track distances from the start node, and handle edge cases such as when start equals target, disconnected graphs, or empty input. They should also explain why BFS guarantees the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

## Code Example
```python
from collections import deque

def shortest_path(graph, start, end):
    if start == end:
        return 0

    queue = deque([(start, 0)])
    visited = {start}

    while queue:
        node, distance = queue.popleft()

        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):
            if neighbor == end:
                return distance + 1
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))

    return -1
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine arrays/linked lists (deques), hash maps, and a heap to maintain a sliding-window top-K efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window, expiration, lazy deletions in a heap
- Per-user state via linked-list/deque and running sums
- Time/space trade-offs for real-time top-K

## Code Example
```javascript
class WaveTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.userMap = new Map(); // user_id -> {queue: [], sum}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // stores [sum, user_id]
  }
  addEvent(userId, score, ts) {
    let entry = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!entry) {
      entry = { queue: [], sum: 0 };
      this.userMap.set(userId, entry);
    }
    entry.queue.push([ts, score]);
    entry.sum += score;
    this.heap.push([entry.sum, userId]);
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const seen = new Map();
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [sum, uid] = this.heap.pop();
      const ent = this.userMap.get(uid);
      if (!ent) continue;
      // expire check
      this.expireUser(uid, currentTime);
      if (ent.sum !== sum) {
        this.heap.push([ent.sum, uid]);
        continue;
      }
      res.push(uid);
    }
    // Reinsert popped elements
    for (const uid of res) this.heap.push([this.userMap.get(uid).sum, uid]);
    return res;
  }
  expireUser(userId, currentTime) {
    const ent = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!ent) return;
    while (ent.queue.length && ent.queue[0][0] <= currentTime - this.W) {
      const [, val] = ent.queue.shift();
      ent.sum -= val;
    }
  }
}
```

